## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
SERS sensor   
Alkaline phosphatase   
MOFs   
Pretreatment-free   
Electrochemical regeneration
Alkaline phosphatase (ALP), widely distributing in the human body, associates with many liver and bone dis­ eases. Herein, we designed a reusable surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) sensor via electrochemical selfassembly of Au nanoparticles (AuNPs) and zinc-based metal organic frameworks (Zn-MOFs) at anodic aluminum oxide (AAO) membrane for detecting ALP activity. The detection is based on the sensitive SERS response of the newly synthesized 2-mercaptoquinone (2-MBQ) modified on AuNPs surface towards ascorbic acid (AA) formed from the dephosphorylation of ascorbic acid 2-phosphate (AA2P) in the presence of ALP. Benefitting from the sieving function of Zn-MOFs and superiority of SERS, the developed sensor exhibited strong anti-interference performance and wide detection range from 1 to ${300}\ \mathrm{U/L}$ with a detection limit of $0.38~\mathrm{U/L}$ , which allowed the direct detection of ALP activity in serum without any pretreatment. Besides, we regenerated the used sensor through the electrochemical oxidation of the reduced probe molecule 2-mercaptohydroquinone (2-MHQ). The regenerated sensor exhibited good maintenance of sensitive response to ALP, in favor of improving the usage efficiency and cutting the test cost. This work provides a promising platform for early diagnosis of ALP-related diseases and paves the way towards developing regenerable and pretreatment-free SERS sensors.
## 1. Introduction
Alkaline phosphatase (ALP), as a glycoprotein that hydrolyzes monophosphate lipids to release inorganic phosphate, widely distributes in human organs like liver, bone, and kidney, in which ALP activity is used for the diagnosis and evaluation of some related diseases, such as jaundice, chronic hepatitis, fracture, and so on [1–5]. Up to now, various methods have been developed for the measurement of ALP activity such as colorimetry, fluorescence, and electrochemical methods [6–9]. Although they have advantages like simple detection procedure and low detection limit, there are still some deficiencies and challenges in the detection of ALP activity, such as poor qualitative analysis ability, weak anti-interference ability, and high detection cost [10–14]. Thus, the development of a highly selective, sensitive, and reproducible detection
technology is very necessary.
As a promising nondestructive testing technology, surface enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) can provide molecular fingerprint information and has advantages of high selectivity, low detection limit, and strong resistance to water interference [15–21]. Based on these advantages, SERS has many applications in the detection of serum components such as uric acid, and various enzymes [22,23]. However, traditional SERS substrates rely on gold or silver colloids that are easy to be affected by interferences and difficult to realize the reusage, restricting the appli­ cation and development of SERS technology in the direct detection of ALP in serum samples [24]. This challenging requires seeking for alternative substrates with high selectivity, stability, and sensitivity for bulk serum sample detections without further dilution or pretreatment.
Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs), since their discovery, have been widely used in catalysis, sensing, separation, and other fields because of its superior porous performance and high specific surface area [25–28]. The combination of MOFs and SERS technology has greatly improved the performance of traditional SERS substrates in terms of detection limit and response time [29,30]. MOFs coated AuNPs could maintain high SERS activity in gas and biological analysis, so as to improve the detection efficiency of SERS sensor [31,32]. However, MOFs in such sensors are usually synthesized by hydrothermal processes that have disadvantages of long fabrication time and nonuniformity [33,34]. While the electrochemical deposition process has advantages of low time cost, in situ growth on the surface of materials, and more controllable morphology and thickness [35–37]. In recent years, the electrochemical deposition of MOFs has been widely used in gas sepa­ ration and sensing applications [38,39]. Nevertheless, the utilization of controllable electrochemical self-assembly MOFs layer on SERS sensor substrate for developing a high selective, sensitive, and reproducible senor, to the best of our knowledge, has not been reported yet for the detection of ALP activity.
In this study, we aimed to design a SERS sensor based on electro­ chemical deposition method for the pretreatment-free detection of ALP activity in serum samples. As shown in Scheme 1, our fabrication method was started with magnetron sputtering of an Au conductive layer on AAO membrane, followed by sequential electrochemical deposition of Au nanoparticles (AuNPs) and Zn-MOFs. Then, the syn­ thesized ascorbic acid (AA)-responsive molecule of 2-mercaptoquinone (2-MBQ) was modified on AuNPs surface to obtain the MOFsfunctionalized SERS sensor. In the presence of ALP, ascorbic acid 2-phos­ phate (AA2P) in solution was dephosphorylated to form AA which reacted with 2-MBQ after dropping the solution on the fabricated sensor, causing the changes in SERS spectra of the sensor. Thus, ALP activity was evaluated with those spectral variations. Profiting from the rapid response of 2-MBQ and pore size screening function of Zn-MOFs, the sensor provided selective and sensitive performance on sensing ALP activity. Due to the reduction of 2-MBQ into 2-MHQ upon detection process and its electrochemical redox properties, the sensor can be conveniently regenerated under applying positive voltage to restore the 2-MBQ for further reusage. The fabricated regenerable sensor exhibited good SERS performance, wide detection range, and strong antiinterference ability for the detection of ALP activity in serum without pretreatment, providing a potential tool for clinical diagnosing of related diseases.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Preparation of MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor
To prepare the MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor, we firstly fabri­ cated an AuNPs/AAO substrate (see supporting information of further details). Thereafter, Zn-MOFs was synthesized on the AuNPs layer of the substrate with a galvanostatic technique in an $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ free precursor solution prepared by stirring solution I (1.5 mmol Zn $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $0.5\mathrm{mmol}$ of ${\bf N a N O}_{3}$ in $5\mathrm{mL}$ DI water) with II (solution II: 0.833 mmol of $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ and
0.9 mmol of CTAB in $\mathtt{5m L}$ ethanol) for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $\mathsf{p H}2.1$ (note: $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was removed from the precursor solution by applying nitrogen flow for $10\mathrm{min}\mathrm{.}$ ) [36]. The fabricated substrate coated by $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs was then washed with $50\%$ ethanol solution and dried with $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ before usage. Finally, $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ solution of 2-MBQ synthesized according to the relevant steps in Scheme S1 was dropped on the bare AAO side to facilitate anchoring the probe molecules at AuNPs layer.
## 2.2. SERS detection of alkaline phosphatase
The ALP lyophilized powder was dissolved with $1\mathrm{mL}$ Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{50~mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}8.0\dot{.}$ ), divided into ten aliquots, and then stored at $20^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to keep its activity. Thereafter, the ALP aliquot was thawed, diluted, and incubated in Tris-HCl buffer $\mathrm{50~mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}8.0\dot{.}$ ) containing $2\mathrm{mM}$ AA2P at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . SERS spectra were collected after the sensor reacted with $60~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the solution for $1\mathrm{min}$ .
A similar method was used to detect ALP activity in real serum samples. The fetal bovine serum (FBS) was directly used without any pretreatment including dilution. ALP was spiked into the FBS samples, where the final concentrations were 20, 50, and $100\mathrm{U/L}$ .
## 2.3. Electrochemical regeneration of MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor
As the detection of ALP led to reducing 2-MBQ at the sensing plat­ form into 2-MHQ, a constant potential of $0.5\mathrm{V}$ was applied for $100\:s$ to oxidize the reduced form and regenerate the used sensor. The regener­ ated sensor was applied for ALP activity detection repeatedly after each regeneration cycle. The regeneration-detection process was carried out for 10 times to investigate the reproducibility of the regenerated sensor towards ALP responsiveness.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor
The AA-responsive 2-MBQ molecules were pre-synthesized to be used as probe molecules for detecting the ALP activity, and the chemical structure was confirmed by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR and mass spectroscopy spectra (Fig. S1, S2).
To enhance the conductivity, a gold film with $40\mathrm{nm}$ thickness was sputtered on the surface of AAO evenly using magnetron sputtering (Fig. 1 A, B). As shown in Fig. 1 C, a uniform and dense AuNPs layer was deposited on the surface of the gold film coated AAO electrochemically, which can provide more “hot spots” and stable enhancement for SERS detection. SEM image and EDS element mapping results proved that ZnMOFs was successfully synthesized on the surface of the AAO through the electrochemical deposition process (Fig. 1 D-H). The elemental composition and crystallography of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs were obtained using EDS and XRD which were consistent with the previously reported works, suggesting no impurities in the unique morphology of MOFs on AuNPs/ AAO substrate (Fig. S3) [36,41]. In addition, nitrogen sorption iso­ therms and pore size distribution indicated that the MOFs layer was mainly mesoporous with a mean pore size of $2.8~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to provide a size exclusion effect for avoiding macromolecular interferences (Fig. S4).
To inspect the stability of the sensor, SERS spectra were collected at different storage times. Interestingly, the intensity of $985\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band preserved a strong stable level with no distinguishable change under 14 days of storage time (Fig. S8), indicating that $Z\mathbf{n}$ -MOFs layer can protect the sensor from the influence of environmental conditions because of the good stability of Zn-MOFs [36].
## 3.2. Conditional optimization of MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor
By investigating the deposition current effect, it could be seen that the growth speed and size of Zn-MOFs layer were significantly increased with the increase of current (Fig. 2 A-D). Typically, at a small current of $1.0\mathrm{mA}$ , MOFs hardly grew. When the current increased to $1.2\mathrm{mA},$ MOFs can grow uniformly on the surface of AAO membrane. However, MOFs grew rapidly at large currents, leading to poor homogeneity. This may be attributed to the increase of current density which increases the nucleation rate of MOFs, causing the change of morphology and ho­ mogeneity. From the SEM images of cross sections, it can be seen that a uniform layer with controllable thickness can be obtained with a smaller current (Fig. S5).
Moreover, the deposition time played a vital role in controlling the morphology of MOFs layer. Within the extension of the time, MOFs crystals first grew horizontally until the surface of AAO was completely covered within $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. 2 E-H). Then the crystals tended to grow vertically and the thickness of MOFs layer was obviously increased (Fig. S5 and S6), which may impair the mass transfer process of the analytes and the propagation of scatter light, thus hindering SERS detection. Therefore, the electrochemical deposition of Zn-MOFs layer was carried out with a current of $1.2\mathrm{mA}$ at $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ for constructing the sensor in the subsequent experiments. We monitored the modification conditions of 2-MBQ on Zn-MOFs/AuNPs/AAO substrate. The band at $985~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ rapidly increased upon increasing the 2-MBQ concentration until reaching $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Fig. S7 A, B). Meanwhile, at higher concentra­ tions the band increased slowly, indicating that the 2-MBQ molecules modified on the surface of AuNPs tended to be saturated. Therefore, a $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ 2-MBQ solution was used for the fabrication of the sensor. Moreover, the SERS intensity at $985~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ reached its maximum within five minutes of the modification time (Fig. S7 C, D), illustrating the optimum time for 2-MBQ molecules to be anchored at the sensor substrate.
## 3.3. Responsivity of MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor towards AA
The presence of ALP led to the formation of AA molecules that correspondingly reduced 2-MBQ into 2-MHQ, resulting in significant changes in the SERS spectrum of the sensor. Specifically, the band at $840~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to $\mathtt{C=}0$ symmetric stretching and CCC ring symmetric stretching and the band at $985~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to $\mathtt{C=}0$ symmetric stretching and CCC ring in-plane deformation gradually decreased with the increasement of AA concentration [40]. Meanwhile, a new band at $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to the C-O symmetric stretching and CCC ring symmetric stretching appeared and gradually increased with the band at $1262\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributed to C-O symmetric stretching and CCC ring in-plane deformation, proving the generation of the reduced form 2-MHQ in the presence of AA [40]. In addition, the band at $470\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ attributes to the symmetric stretching of the CCC ring was shifted to $476~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ without distinguishable intensity change. Based on the inde­ pendence of $470\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band, a quantitative detection was performed using the relative ratio of the peak intensity at $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $\mathsf{i}70\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{I}_{900}/\mathrm{I}_{470})$ . Inspired from these sensitive vibrational fingerprint changes, we applied our sensor for AA detection. The sensor provided a fast response to AA with rapid increase of $\mathrm{I}_{900}/\mathrm{I}_{470}$ intensity which reached plateau within 1 min (Fig. 3A, B), representing the fast SERS response of our sensor towards AA. In addition, as can be seen from Fig. 3C and D, $\mathrm{I}_{900}/\mathrm{I}_{470}$ linearly increased as AA concentration increased from $30~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $1\mathrm{mM}$ . These results demonstrated that our sensor has a potential high sensitivity with a fast and wide response for the detection of ALP activity based on AA sensing.
## 3.4. Sensing performance of MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor for ALP activity
We designed a strategy to detect ALP activity based on its dephos­ phorylation effect. In the presence of ALP, AA is produced by removing the phosphate group of AA2P. Thus, AA could cross the MOFs layer and react with 2-MBQ, resulting in representative changes in the SERS spectrum while some macromolecular substances like enzymes are blocked out. Based on this, SERS response of the sensor to the concen­ trations of ALP was investigated, the peak intensity at $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corre­ spondingly increased with the increase of ALP concentration (Fig. 4A). What’s more, a roughly linear relationship was established based on ratio metric peak intensity of $\mathrm{I}_{900}/\mathrm{I}_{470}$ and the logarithmic concentra­ tions of ALP $(1{-}300~\mathrm{U/L})$ with calculated limit of detection $0.38\mathrm{U}/\mathrm{L}$ according to a signal-to-noise ratio of 1:3 (Fig. 4 B), which is superior with other reported methods (Table S1).
Furthermore, we compared the anti-jamming performance of sensors with and without MOFs layer by measuring the responsiveness of the sensors in the presence of some interferences including $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ , GGT,
GOD, etc. These interferences were added respectively with $100\mathrm{U/L}$ ALP in Tris-HCl $\mathrm{(pH~8.0)}$ solution containing $2\mathrm{mM}$ AA2P, and were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ before SERS measurement. Despite the presence of interferences, the SERS signals obtained by the MOFsfunctionalized SERS sensor still had a consistent performance on the band at $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4C). However, for sensor without the MOFs layer, the peak intensity at $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ largely varied in the presence of different distractors (Fig. 4 E). As shown in Fig. $^4\mathrm{~D~}$ , the $\mathrm{I}_{900}/\mathrm{I}_{470}$ in­ tensities were more persistent in the presence of the MOFs layer with small error bars. In contrast, the $\mathrm{I}_{900}/\mathrm{I}_{470}$ intensities in case of the bare sensor without MOFs layer had more fluctuations that affected the sensor accuracy (Fig. 4F). It is reasonable to believe that presence of MOFs layer provided an efficient interference-free approach with high selectivity for the direct detection of ALP activity in serum samples.
## 3.5. Direct detection of alkaline phosphatase in serum samples
Intrigued from the superior performance and anti-interference abil­ ity of the MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor in the previous sections, we directly detected the ALP activity in FBS samples without any dilution or pretreatment. Although the serum contains many enzymes, proteins, fats, inorganic salts, and other substances which would have serious interference to the detection process, the SERS spectral response at $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ showed the reliability of our sensor for detecting ALP at different spiked serum samples (Fig. S9 A). The $\mathrm{I}_{900}/\mathrm{I}_{470}$ intensities collected with the sensor (Fig. S9 B) were used to calculate the recovered ALP concentrations through the linear curve in Fig. 4 B. Table 1 lists the results of the MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor for detecting ALP ac­ tivity in undiluted FBS, demonstrating a good recovery percentage from $98\%$ to $104\%$ with low RSD. These results further indicated that the MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor had a great potential for the reliable detection of ALP activity in real serum samples without any required pretreatment.
## 3.6. Electrochemical regeneration of MOFs-functionalized SERS sensor
As 2-MBQ/2-MHQ can be reversibly transformed using electrochemical-driven process, we investigated the voltametric behavior of our sensor. As shown in the CV curves (Fig. 5A), the modi­ fication of 2-MBQ on the sensor led to appearing of a redox peak in the represented voltammogram, demonstrating the capability of the sensor to be regenerated electrochemically. Moreover, the existence of MOFs layer caused enhancement in the Faradaic current compared with the sensor without MOFs, suggesting a potential improvement on the response performances. To ensure regeneration of the sensor, a higher voltage of $0.5\mathrm{V}$ was applied, while SERS spectra were collected from the used MOFs-functionalized SERS senor during the regeneration process at different times (Fig. 5 B). Interestingly, under the applied potential, the characteristic peak of 2-MBQ at $985\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be recovered within $^{100s,}$ meanwhile the band at $900~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was faded. Based on these re­ sults, a detection-regeneration strategy was carried out for 10 times to evaluate the electrochemical renewability of the sensor. The reversible SERS spectral changes during the recycling process indicated a good reproducibility which the regenerated sensor could maintain good ALP responsiveness for several regeneration cycles (Fig. 5C, D). Moreover, the morphology and structure of MOFs were still intact after 10 times detection-regeneration cycles (Fig. S10), further suggesting that the sensor is stable to the changes of solution environment. Taking together, the rapid electrochemical regeneration and the stable reproducibility provide further insights for the usage of our sensor as a promising biosensor.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, a MOFs-functionalized reusable SERS sensor was developed for sensing ALP activity based on electrochemistry. Using electrochemical deposition technology, AuNPs and MOFs can grow in situ on the AAO membrane with high controllable fabrication unifor­ mity, which could help to improve the reproducibility of SERS detection. Benefiting from the ultra-sensitivity of SERS and specificity of ALP catalyzed reaction, the sensor exhibited a good sensing performance and provided a low detection limit with a wide detection range. Meanwhile, the sieve effect of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs and specific response of 2-MBQ greatly improved the anti-interference ability and repeatability for the direct detection of ALP activity in real serum samples without pretreatment, highlighting the importance of MOFs functionalization in the sensor response compared to the previous reported platforms. Based on the redox behavior of 2-MBQ, the sensor can expediently realize multiple electrochemical regenerations and still maintained the integrity of structure and sensitive response to ALP, which could cut the detection cost and provided a possibility for unmanned SERS analysis. The pro­ posed approach in this study offers a strategy to develop recyclable SERS sensor for the rapid and sensitive pretreatment-free detection of ALP activity in serum samples, which shows promising prospects in the diagnosis and analysis of its related diseases.
